==============================
Building and Installing Pure
==============================

.. highlight:: console

Pure uses Clang/LLVM 22 as its compiler toolchain and JIT backend. The supported
source build uses CMake and Ninja. The Autoconf and GNU make files in older
releases are legacy infrastructure and are not the supported development path.

Supported Toolchain
===================

The reference configuration is tested on 64-bit Linux with:

- CMake 3.25 or newer and Ninja;
- Clang 22 and LLVM 22 development files, including ``llvm-config-22``;
- Bison 3.0 or newer and Flex 2.6 or newer;
- GMP, MPFR, GNU readline, and PCRE POSIX development packages;
- POSIX threads, dynamic loading, and iconv support.

The CMake configuration checks that the C and C++ compilers and the LLVM CMake
package are all version 22. Set ``PURE_STRICT_TOOLCHAIN=OFF`` only for
exploratory unsupported builds.

Optional tools enable additional validation or integrations:

- Faust builds the DSP lifecycle fixtures;
- GSL enables examples which use the GNU Scientific Library;
- Flang 22 and GFortran enable Fortran bitcode fixtures and examples;
- Sphinx supports external documentation generation workflows (the CMake build
  does not currently provide a documentation target);
- LLDB 22 and ``lldb-dap-22`` provide command-line and Zed debugging.

Other operating systems and architectures have not yet completed the LLVM 22
release-validation matrix. Report portability issues with the configure output,
compiler versions, and failing command.

Preset Build
============

The repository presets select the reference LLVM 22 tools and use separate
build directories::

  $ cmake --preset llvm22-debug
  $ cmake --build --preset llvm22-debug --parallel 1

Use the Release preset for an optimized build::

  $ cmake --preset llvm22-release
  $ cmake --build --preset llvm22-release --parallel 1

``--parallel 1`` is recommended on memory-constrained systems because compiling
LLVM-heavy translation units can require substantial memory.

The sanitizer configuration enables AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer::

  $ cmake --preset llvm22-asan
  $ cmake --build --preset llvm22-asan --parallel 1

A custom build can use the same requirements without presets::

  $ cmake -S . -B build/release -G Ninja \
      -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang-22 \
      -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++-22 \
      -DLLVM_DIR=/usr/lib/llvm-22/lib/cmake/llvm
  $ cmake --build build/release --parallel 1

Test Suite
==========

Run the focused integration suite in a configured build with::

  $ ctest --preset llvm22-debug -E pure-regression --output-on-failure

The complete preset also runs the historical regression corpus::

  $ ctest --preset llvm22-debug --output-on-failure
  $ ctest --preset llvm22-release --output-on-failure
  $ ctest --preset llvm22-asan --output-on-failure

The regression runner starts a fresh interpreter for each script and keeps up to
four processes active. CTest budgets the complete corpus for 10 minutes in Release,
15 minutes in Debug, and 30 minutes under ASan/UBSan. Observed LLVM 22 validation
times on a 12-CPU host were approximately 5, 8, and 22 minutes respectively.
Sanitizer regression runs disable Pure's address-difference stack heuristic because
ASan detects actual stack overflow, and use a nonzero 64 MiB quarantine to retain
use-after-free coverage without multiplying memory by the ASan default for every
worker. To include each script's elapsed wall time in the otherwise ordered runner
output, invoke ``run-tests -t`` in a build directory or set ``TEST_TIMINGS=1``.

Leak checks use the ASan build and are intended for focused tests::

  $ ctest --preset llvm22-lsan -R pure-jit-lifetime-stress \
      --output-on-failure

No sanitizer finding should be suppressed without a documented root cause.

Installation
============

Choose the final prefix at configure time so that ``pure.pc`` and Pure's library
search path contain the installed location::

  $ cmake --preset llvm22-release -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr/local
  $ cmake --build --preset llvm22-release --parallel 1
  $ sudo cmake --install build/llvm22-release

For an unprivileged or staging installation, configure a separate build::

  $ cmake -S . -B build/stage -G Ninja \
      -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang-22 \
      -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++-22 \
      -DLLVM_DIR=/usr/lib/llvm-22/lib/cmake/llvm \
      -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
  $ cmake --build build/stage --parallel 1
  $ cmake --install build/stage

The core installation contains:

- the ``pure`` interpreter and versioned ``libpure`` runtime;
- ``runtime.h``, ``pure_main.c``, and ``pure_main.o`` for extensions and embedding;
- the standard Pure library scripts;
- ``pure.pc`` pkg-config metadata; and
- the ``pure(1)`` manual page.

A nonstandard runtime library directory may need to be added to the platform's
dynamic-loader search path. On Linux this can be done temporarily with
``LD_LIBRARY_PATH`` or permanently through the system loader configuration.

After installation, verify the selected toolchain and package metadata::

  $ pure --version
  $ pkg-config --modversion pure
  $ pure examples/hello.pure

Optional editor integrations are disabled by default. Enable their CMake
installation rules when configuring a package or dedicated user installation::

  $ cmake --preset llvm22-release \
      -DPURE_INSTALL_EMACS_MODE=ON \
      -DPURE_INSTALL_TEXMACS_PLUGIN=ON

The Emacs Lisp files default to ``share/emacs/site-lisp`` and the TeXmacs tree
to ``share/TeXmacs`` below the installation prefix. Override these locations
with ``PURE_EMACS_SITE_LISP_DIR`` and ``PURE_TEXMACS_DIR``. CMake installs
portable Emacs Lisp source; package maintainers may byte-compile it for their
target Emacs version after installation.

Uninstallation
==============

CMake records installed files in ``install_manifest.txt``. Remove exactly those
files with the build's ``uninstall`` target::

  $ sudo cmake --build build/llvm22-release --target uninstall

The target is idempotent and leaves empty parent directories in place. It must
use the build tree that performed the installation.

Debugging
=========

Debug builds retain frame pointers and debugger-compatible DWARF information.
The repository contains a Zed launch profile named ``Pure JIT smoke (LLDB 22)``
which uses ``/usr/bin/lldb-dap-22`` and builds serially before launch.

The command-line equivalent is::

  $ lldb-22 -- build/llvm22-debug/pure-jit-smoke

Useful symbolic breakpoints include ``PureJit::create``,
``PureJit::add_module_copy``, ``PureJit::lookup``,
``interpreter::compile_orc_function``, and
``CompilationUnitResources::remove_all``. Linux Debug builds publish generated
function names through LLVM's GDB JIT interface. For example::

  (lldb) breakpoint set --name pure_jit_smoke_value
  (lldb) run

Set ``PURE_JIT_DUMP=ir``, ``objects``, or ``ir,objects`` to print opt-in ORC
diagnostics. These modes are silent by default and never print host addresses.

LLVM Bitcode Policy
===================

Bitcode imported with ``using "bc:module"`` must be produced by a compatible
LLVM 22 frontend for the interpreter's target architecture and data layout.
For C and C++, use Clang 22::

  $ clang-22 -O2 -emit-llvm -c module.c -o module.bc

The loader rejects malformed input and architecture, endianness, or data-layout
mismatches before publication. Public definitions are namespaced internally,
duplicate exports are diagnosed, unresolved dependencies report their symbol
names, and module resources are released with the owning interpreter unit.

Bitcode is not a stable cross-version interchange format. Rebuild modules with
the release's LLVM major version instead of reusing artifacts from LLVM 2.x,
3.x, or another target.

Faust Policy
============

Faust integration consumes LLVM 22-compatible bitcode. The recommended direct
command is::

  $ faust -double -lang llvm example.dsp -o example.bc

The bundled ``pure.c`` architecture can also be compiled with Clang 22::

  $ faust -double -a pure.c -lang c example.dsp -o example.c
  $ clang-22 -O3 -emit-llvm -c example.c -o example.bc

Current Faust modules expose an explicit ``pure_faust_sample_format`` marker.
Pure validates this marker, falls back to recognized legacy metadata only when
necessary, and rejects unknown sample ABIs instead of guessing. Reload is
transactional: the old generation remains available if validation or
materialization fails, and live DSP instances prevent unsafe replacement.

Development Workflow
====================

Make changes in a dedicated branch, build with ``--parallel 1``, and start with
the narrowest relevant CTest selection before broader validation. Do not add new
Autoconf checks or LLVM version macros. New LLVM integration should use the
CMake package and ORC APIs already wrapped by ``PureJit``.
